  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 218 AM EDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nTuesday and Wednesday, the main feature of interest for our weather \nacross central KY and southern IN will be a meandering cutoff low \ninitially over Georgia Tuesday morning. To the north of this \nfeature, shortwave ridging is expected to pass across the midwest on \nTuesday, keeping the low and associated richer moisture suppressed \nto the southeast. Perhaps a few showers or a storm could develop for \nareas closer to the Cumberland Plateau Tuesday afternoon; however, \nHREF probabilities of measurable precipitation are only 10-15% at \nthis time. Otherwise, dry, mostly sunny conditions are expected, \nwith highs generally in the mid 80s.\n\nTuesday night into Wednesday, an approaching shortwave on the \nleading edge of broader troughing over central Canada is expected to \nbegin to draw the closed low northward toward eastern KY, bringing \nhigher PWATs (1.6-1.8\") and sfc dewpoints with it. This should \nsupport greater chances for daytime heating-driven showers and \nthunderstorms, mainly east of I-65, where 40-60% coverage is \nexpected. The flow aloft will still be seasonably weak, so would \nexpect mainly single cell summertime showers and storms, with severe \nweather very unlikely. Temperatures will be nearly identical to \nTuesday, if not a degree or two above persistence.\n\nThe broad trough over the north central US and central Canada should \nabsorb the closed low by Thursday, resulting in an upper level \npattern with ridging across the western CONUS and along the Atlantic \nseaboard with baggy troughing across the Midwest. This pattern \nshould persist Thursday through the first part of next weekend, \nallowing a sfc cold front to approach the region from the northwest. \nThere is some timing discrepancy between medium-range ensembles, but \nin general, Thursday is still expected to feature a relative minimum \nin precipitation coverage, with shower/storm chances increasing \nThursday night into Friday. Modest mid-level flow should increase \ndeep-layer shear to around 20-25 knots late week, potentially \nsupporting a few multicell clusters. Additionally, a slight increase \nin moisture should lead to somewhat higher instability. However, \ngenerally speaking, the parameter space favors more garden-variety \nstorms with potentially a strong storm or two Friday into Saturday. \nTemperatures should approach climatology for early August by late \nweek, with highs ranging from the mid 80s to around 90.\n\nWith the upper trough not currently expected to amplify late week \ninto next weekend, the sfc cold front should hang up to the north of \nthe area next weekend. Continued seasonably warm and humid \nconditions are expected through next weekend. Shower/storm chances \nare expected to become more isolated Sunday as the cold front washes \nout.",
